From: HONOR ZQC-P Linux fixes <noreply@example.invalid>
Date: Fri, 29 May 2026 23:50:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] platform/x86: huawei-wmi: Storm-detection for KEY_MICMUTE
 (WMI 0x287)

On some HONOR MagicBook and Huawei MateBook EC firmware revisions, the
embedded controller autonomously fires the mic-privacy WMI event 0x287
in 2-event pairs separated by 0.5–5 seconds ("privacy storm"), without
any user keypress. Confirmed reproduction on HONOR MagicBook Pro 14 AI
(ZQC-P / M1010, Intel Core Ultra X9 388H, Panther Lake) running
linux-cachyos 7.0.10: 17 storm bursts over an 80-minute session,
triggered by various combinations of touchpad swipes, focus changes to
audio-playing windows, intel_lpmd platform-profile transitions, and
even pure idle. The EC also writes the legacy PS/2 scancode 0xf8 to
i8042 in parallel, which surfaces in dmesg as
  atkbd serio0: Unknown key pressed (translated set 2, code 0xf8
  on isa0060/serio0).

Each pair toggles the default source mute twice, which would normally
self-cancel; but in practice userspace dispatch races (Wayland
compositor + gsd-media-keys grab + audio backend) leave the state
inconsistent, with the platform::micmute LED stuck on and the
microphone silently muted until the user manually unmutes via the
tray icon or pavucontrol. The user-visible symptom is "mic mutes
itself spontaneously and the Fn+F7 hardware shortcut no longer
restores it."

The root cause is in the EC firmware and the trigger is not exposed
via any documented ACPI/WMI dispatch method (the full WMAA dispatcher
in SSDT21 was audited — only the OS->EC setter SMLS touches the mic
state, and no "disable privacy timer" setter exists). HONOR's own
Windows PCManager binaries do not call any such setter either —
Windows is simply fast enough that the 2-event self-cancel returns
mute to the original state without a visible LED flicker.

Detect the storm by distinguishing a legitimate single Fn+F7 press
(one event) from a spurious EC storm pair (two events within a
configurable window) at the driver level:

  1. On the first 0x287, defer emission by micmute_storm_window_ms.
  2. If a second 0x287 arrives during the window, drop both (storm).
  3. Otherwise emit the deferred event normally (single press).

The new module parameter micmute_storm_window_ms (default 1000 ms,
0 = disable) is writable at runtime so the trade-off (a <=1 s emit
latency on legitimate Fn+F7) can be tuned per machine. All other WMI
events (volume, brightness, wlan, ...) keep upstream immediate-emit
behaviour.

Tested-on: HONOR MagicBook Pro 14 AI ZQC-P / M1010 (1ee7:209d)
Signed-off-by: HONOR ZQC-P Linux fixes <noreply@example.invalid>
---
 drivers/platform/x86/huawei-wmi.c | 63 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 63 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/platform/x86/huawei-wmi.c b/drivers/platform/x86/huawei-wmi.c
--- a/drivers/platform/x86/huawei-wmi.c
+++ b/drivers/platform/x86/huawei-wmi.c
@@ -17,6 +17,7 @@
 #include <linux/platform_device.h>
 #include <linux/power_supply.h>
 #include <linux/sysfs.h>
+#include <linux/workqueue.h>
 #include <linux/wmi.h>
 #include <acpi/battery.h>
 
@@ -104,6 +105,64 @@
 MODULE_PARM_DESC(report_brightness,
 		"Report brightness keys.");
 
+/*
+ * KEY_MICMUTE storm detection — see commit message for full background.
+ *
+ * Some HONOR / Huawei EC firmwares emit the mic-privacy WMI event
+ * 0x287 in autonomous 2-event pairs ("privacy storm"). We defer the
+ * first 0x287 by micmute_storm_window_ms; if a second arrives during
+ * the window we drop both. Set the parameter to 0 to restore the
+ * upstream immediate-emit behaviour.
+ */
+static int micmute_storm_window_ms = 1000;
+module_param(micmute_storm_window_ms, int, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(micmute_storm_window_ms,
+		"EC privacy-storm window (ms) for KEY_MICMUTE (0x287); 0 disables.");
+
+static atomic_t huawei_wmi_micmute_pending = ATOMIC_INIT(0);
+static struct input_dev *huawei_wmi_micmute_idev;
+static const struct key_entry *huawei_wmi_micmute_key;
+
+static void huawei_wmi_micmute_emit_deferred(struct work_struct *work);
+static DECLARE_DELAYED_WORK(huawei_wmi_micmute_work,
+			    huawei_wmi_micmute_emit_deferred);
+
+static void huawei_wmi_micmute_emit_deferred(struct work_struct *work)
+{
+	if (atomic_xchg(&huawei_wmi_micmute_pending, 0) &&
+	    huawei_wmi_micmute_idev && huawei_wmi_micmute_key)
+		sparse_keymap_report_entry(huawei_wmi_micmute_idev,
+					   huawei_wmi_micmute_key, 1, true);
+}
+
+/* Returns true when the event was consumed by storm-detection. */
+static bool huawei_wmi_micmute_filter(struct input_dev *idev,
+				      const struct key_entry *key)
+{
+	int window_ms = READ_ONCE(micmute_storm_window_ms);
+
+	if (window_ms <= 0)
+		return false;
+
+	if (atomic_xchg(&huawei_wmi_micmute_pending, 1)) {
+		/*
+		 * Second event arrived within the window — storm pair,
+		 * drop both. cancel_delayed_work() is safe to call from
+		 * the WMI notify context (it does not wait).
+		 */
+		cancel_delayed_work(&huawei_wmi_micmute_work);
+		atomic_set(&huawei_wmi_micmute_pending, 0);
+		return true;
+	}
+
+	/* First event — defer to see if a storm sibling arrives. */
+	huawei_wmi_micmute_idev = idev;
+	huawei_wmi_micmute_key = key;
+	schedule_delayed_work(&huawei_wmi_micmute_work,
+			      msecs_to_jiffies(window_ms));
+	return true;
+}
+
 /* Quirks */
 
 static int __init dmi_matched(const struct dmi_system_id *dmi)
@@ -735,6 +794,9 @@
 			key->sw.code == KEY_BRIGHTNESSUP))
 		return;
 
+	if (code == 0x287 && huawei_wmi_micmute_filter(idev, key))
+		return;
+
 	sparse_keymap_report_entry(idev, key, 1, true);
 }
 
@@ -780,6 +842,7 @@
 
 static void huawei_wmi_input_exit(struct device *dev, const char *guid)
 {
+	cancel_delayed_work_sync(&huawei_wmi_micmute_work);
 	wmi_remove_notify_handler(guid);
 }
 
